{
  "validity": "pass",
  "quality": "strong",
  "issues": [
    {
      "kind": "access",
      "location": "access_plan / obligations.workspaces_with_oncall_admin",
      "problem": "users.info takes no team_id parameter and its documented response shape does not specify whether a returned workspace-membership role is tagged with the team it applies to. The Summit negative depends on the solver being able to tell that Priyanka Rao's admin role belongs to T02NORTHSTAR and not to T03SUMMIT when she is looked up while investigating Summit's on-call channel.",
      "repair": "Confirm (or have the compiler confirm) that users.info responses include the associated team_id alongside the role, so the 'same workspace' comparison is actually verifiable from API output rather than assumed; if not, add a documented note in the access plan on how the single-membership convention resolves this instead of relying on it implicitly."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The route (WORKSPACE→CONVERSATION→CONVERSATION_MEMBERSHIP→USER→WORKSPACE_MEMBERSHIP) is fully exercised and matches the prompt's 'on-call channel that includes a workspace admin' phrasing exactly, with the binding_note correctly requiring same-workspace equality rather than any-workspace admin status. resolution_mode 'multiple' is justified by two genuine matches (Northstar, Lumen). Negatives are well-differentiated and semantically meaningful rather than keyword-based: wrong exact channel name (incident-response), no admin in workspace at all (Vertex), admin present but split across different channels in the same workspace (base Test Workspace), and admin present but the same person's admin role belongs to a different workspace (Summit) — this last is the most demanding case and is exactly the kind of edge case the review criteria call out (membership vs. authorship-style distinctions, verifying requested equality is actually represented). Data roles are consistent with the base seed (existing base admins reused correctly) and new rows are plan-stage additions the compiler will create, which is permitted. Only open question is whether the users.info access step can concretely resolve the same-workspace check given the documented API surface; this is flagged but does not by itself invalidate the design given the runtime_qualifications explicitly anticipate single-workspace-per-user determinism for this exact reason."
}
